<section #sh-a | `<a>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *a*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<a>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<a>`

<code #code:sh-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-1>
See <a https://example.com/docs | the documentation>.
</minipage>

*The positional URL form — the target is the first argument, the pipe content is the link text. The most common authoring path. (Markdown `[text](url)` is not an enscribe idiom; it renders as literal text.)*

<code #code:sh-a-2>
<a href=https://example.com | the example site>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-2>
<a href=https://example.com | the example site>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-a-3>
<a href=https://example.com target=_blank rel=noopener | external link>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-3>
<a href=https://example.com target=_blank rel=noopener | external link>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-a-4>
<a href=#section-2 | jump to Section 2>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-a-4>
<a href=#section-2 | jump to Section 2>
</minipage>

*Internal links use fragment identifiers pointing at element ids.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `href` | kwarg |  | The URL or fragment identifier the anchor points to. Required for hyperlinks; optional for anchors used as link targets. |
| `target` | kwarg | `_self` `_blank` `_parent` `_top` | How the link opens. _blank opens in a new tab/window. |
| `rel` | kwarg |  | Relationship between the current document and the link target. Common values: nofollow, noopener, noreferrer, external. |
| `title` | kwarg |  | Tooltip text shown on hover. |

</section>

<section #sh-abbr | `<abbr>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *abbr*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<abbr>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<abbr>`

<code #code:sh-abbr-1>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-abbr-1>
The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM> is the browser API for HTML.
</minipage>

*Standard pattern — abbreviation with its expansion as the title kwarg. Browsers show the expansion in a hover tooltip.*

<code #code:sh-abbr-2>
Using <abbr | CSS> selectors.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-abbr-2>
Using <abbr | CSS> selectors.
</minipage>

*Bare abbreviation, no title. Acceptable when the expansion has already been introduced earlier in the document.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | kwarg |  | The abbreviation's expansion. Standard HTML <abbr title="..."> — browsers display the expansion as a tooltip on hover. Strongly recommended on first use of an abbreviation; optional on subsequent uses if the expansion is already in scope. |

</section>

<section #sh-b | `<b>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *b*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<b>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<b>`
- Markdown: `**…**`

<code #code:sh-b-1>
The keyword <b type=keyword | recursion> is fundamental.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-b-1>
The keyword <b type=keyword | recursion> is fundamental.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-b-2>
Use <b type=product-name | Acrobat> to read the file.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-b-2>
Use <b type=product-name | Acrobat> to read the file.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `keyword` `product-name` `lead` `offset` `other` |  |

</section>

<section #sh-em | `<em>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *em*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<em>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<em>`

<code #code:sh-em-1>
This has *emphasized* content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-1>
This has *emphasized* content.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown emphasis with single asterisks (or single underscores) produces &lt;em&gt; elements. This is the most common authoring path.*

<code #code:sh-em-2>
<em | emphasized>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-2>
<em | emphasized>
</minipage>

*The explicit form is reached for when attributes are needed.*

<code #code:sh-em-3>
<em #key-term .highlighted | distinguishing feature>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-em-3>
<em #key-term .highlighted | distinguishing feature>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #sh-i | `<i>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *i*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<i>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<i>`
- Markdown: `*…*  (or _…_)`

<code #code:sh-i-1>
The species is <i type=taxonomic | Loxodonta africana>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-1>
The species is <i type=taxonomic | Loxodonta africana>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-i-2>
The French <i type=foreign | tour de force> is impressive.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-2>
The French <i type=foreign | tour de force> is impressive.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-i-3>
The technical term <i type=technical | mitochondria> refers to organelles.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-i-3>
The technical term <i type=technical | mitochondria> refers to organelles.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `foreign` `taxonomic` `technical` `thought` `ship-name` `other` | Optional classification of the italic's role. Useful for accessibility tools and stylesheet targeting. |

</section>

<section #sh-kbd | `<kbd>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *kbd*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<kbd>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<kbd>`

<code #code:sh-kbd-1>
Press <kbd | Ctrl+C> to copy.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-kbd-1>
Press <kbd | Ctrl+C> to copy.
</minipage>

*Single chord as a kbd block. Browsers render &lt;kbd&gt; in a monospace font by default, distinguishing it from surrounding prose.*

<code #code:sh-kbd-2>
Press <kbd | <kbd | Ctrl>+<kbd | C>> to copy.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-kbd-2>
Press <kbd | <kbd | Ctrl>+<kbd | C>> to copy.
</minipage>

*Nested &lt;kbd&gt; distinguishes individual keys in a chord. Browsers render the outer block as the chord and the inner blocks as individual keys, both monospace.*

</section>

<section #sh-marginnote | `<marginnote>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *marginnote*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<aside>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<marginnote>`

<code #code:sh-marginnote-1>
The result holds.<marginnote | A caveat, set in the margin.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-marginnote-1>
The result holds.<marginnote | A caveat, set in the margin.>
</minipage>

*An unnumbered margin aside, authored in place. Unlike a numbered &lt;note&gt;, it is not collected, numbered, or relocated — it renders where written and floats into the margin column (note-position is irrelevant to it).*

<code #code:sh-marginnote-2>
<marginnote #m1 | A margin note with an id.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-marginnote-2>
<marginnote #m1 | A margin note with an id.>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #sh-output | `<output>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *output*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<output>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<output>`

<code #code:sh-output-1>
The function returns <output | 42> for the test input.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-output-1>
The function returns <output | 42> for the test input.
</minipage>

*Result of a calculation in prose. &lt;output&gt; is semantically distinct from &lt;samp&gt; — &lt;samp&gt; is what a program prints (a display artifact); &lt;output&gt; is the result of a computation (a semantic value). Browsers render both similarly.*

</section>

<section #sh-q | `<q>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *q*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<q>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<q>`

<code #code:sh-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-q-1>
She said <q | hello> in passing.
</minipage>

*Browsers automatically render &lt;q&gt; with quotation marks. Authors do not include quotation marks in the content.*

<code #code:sh-q-2>
The phrase <q cite=https://example.com | to be or not to be> is iconic.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-q-2>
The phrase <q cite=https://example.com | to be or not to be> is iconic.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cite` | kwarg |  | URL of the source being quoted. Same as <blockquote>'s cite attribute but for inline quotations. |

</section>

<section #sh-s | `<s>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *s*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<s>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<s>`
- Markdown: `~~…~~`

<code #code:sh-s-1>
The price is ~~$50~~ now $40.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-s-1>
The price is ~~$50~~ now $40.
</minipage>

*GFM's tilde syntax produces &lt;s&gt; elements. The most common authoring path for casual strikethrough.*

<code #code:sh-s-2>
The claim <s type=retracted | was unsupported> has been corrected.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-s-2>
The claim <s type=retracted | was unsupported> has been corrected.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `outdated` `retracted` `deleted` `other` |  |

</section>

<section #sh-samp | `<samp>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *samp*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<samp>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<samp>`

<code #code:sh-samp-1>
The command prints <samp | Hello, world!> to stdout.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-samp-1>
The command prints <samp | Hello, world!> to stdout.
</minipage>

*Sample output from a program. Browsers render &lt;samp&gt; in a monospace font by default, distinguishing it from surrounding prose.*

<code #code:sh-samp-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-samp-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</minipage>

*&lt;samp&gt; for the sample value paired with &lt;var&gt; for the variable name — the natural pair for documenting configuration in technical writing.*

</section>

<section #sh-span | `<span>`>

`<span>` is no longer an Enscribe element. An author-typed `<span>` is treated like any other unknown tag: it prints **literally**, exactly as typed. `<span | text>` appears on the page as the characters `<span | text>`, not as a compiled element.

For styled or marked inline text, use the semantic inline element that fits the meaning instead — `<em>`, `<strong>`, `<i>`, `<b>`, `<u>`, `<s>`, `<sub>`, `<sup>`, `<q>`, `<abbr>`, `<kbd>`, `<var>`, `<samp>`, `<output>`, `<code>`, `<term>`, or `<cite>` (each documented above). Each carries its own meaning and class hooks, so it styles cleanly without a generic wrapper.

</section>

<section #sh-strong | `<strong>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *strong*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<strong>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<strong>`

<code #code:sh-strong-1>
This is **strongly emphasized** content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-1>
This is **strongly emphasized** content.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown with double asterisks (or double underscores) produces &lt;strong&gt; elements.*

<code #code:sh-strong-2>
<strong | important>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-2>
<strong | important>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-strong-3>
<strong #critical .warning | This is critical.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-strong-3>
<strong #critical .warning | This is critical.>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #sh-sub | `<sub>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sub*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<sub>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sub>`

<code #code:sh-sub-1>
Water is H<sub | 2>O.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-1>
Water is H<sub | 2>O.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sub-2>
The vector x<sub | i> represents the i-th component.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sub-2>
The vector x<sub | i> represents the i-th component.
</minipage>

</section>

<section #sh-sup | `<sup>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *sup*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<sup>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<sup>`

<code #code:sh-sup-1>
The 1<sup | st> edition of the work.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-1>
The 1<sup | st> edition of the work.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sup-2>
The function f(x) = x<sup | 2>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-2>
The function f(x) = x<sup | 2>.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-sup-3>
The isotope <sup | 12>C is abundant.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-sup-3>
The isotope <sup | 12>C is abundant.
</minipage>

</section>

<section #sh-term | `<term>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *term*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<term>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<term>`

<code #code:sh-term-1>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-term-1>
An <term | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation.
</minipage>

*Standard term introduction. The element marks the word as "this is a term being introduced," typically rendered in italic or bold by default CSS.*

<code #code:sh-term-2>
An <term #term:eigenvector | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation. Later we generalize <term | eigenvector>s to operators.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-term-2>
An <term #term:eigenvector | eigenvector> is a non-zero vector that scales under a linear transformation. Later we generalize <term | eigenvector>s to operators.
</minipage>

*First introduction carries an id so later references can link back to it. Subsequent uses of the same term (without an id) still mark it as a term being referenced, distinct from running prose, without re-asserting the introduction.*

</section>

<section #sh-u | `<u>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *u*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<u>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<u>`

<code #code:sh-u-1>
The author wrote <u type=misspelling | recieve> in the original.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-u-1>
The author wrote <u type=misspelling | recieve> in the original.
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-u-2>
The Chinese name <u type=proper-name | 王明> appears here.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-u-2>
The Chinese name <u type=proper-name | 王明> appears here.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `misspelling` `proper-name` `editorial-correction` `other` |  |

</section>

<section #sh-var | `<var>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *var*. Compiles to Layer 1 `<var>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<var>`

<code #code:sh-var-1>
The function takes a parameter <var | n> and returns <var | n>²+1.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-var-1>
The function takes a parameter <var | n> and returns <var | n>²+1.
</minipage>

*Variable names in prose. Browsers render &lt;var&gt; in italic by default, distinguishing it from surrounding prose.*

<code #code:sh-var-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-var-2>
Set <var | threshold> to <samp | 0.05>.
</minipage>

*&lt;var&gt; for the variable name and &lt;samp&gt; for a sample value — the natural pair for documenting configuration in technical writing.*

</section>

